The fix is to put the caller's account into the `WHERE` clause of each write. The database then enforces ownership as part of the update itself, and a request for someone else's id matches zero rows.

## What's wrong

`sessionUser` (`auth.js:11`) already sets `req.user.accountId`, but none of the three writes use it:

- `routes/accounts.js:8`: any logged-in user can `PATCH /accounts/<any id>` and change another tenant's `billing_email`. That's the worst of the three, because it lets them redirect someone else's invoices.
- `routes/accounts.js:15`: any logged-in user can soft-delete any account.
- `routes/orders.js:16`: any logged-in user can cancel any order.

The routes also ignore the case where nothing matches. `db.write` returns `res.rows[0]`, which is `undefined` when no row is updated, so the handlers reply 200 with an empty body or 204. Once the writes are scoped, "not yours" will look exactly like "no match," so the handlers need to check for that.

## The fix

**`routes/orders.js`**: add `account_id` to the filter and return 404 when no row comes back:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

**`routes/accounts.js`**: the only account a user may change is their own, so the URL id has to equal `req.user.accountId`. I'd bind both values in SQL rather than compare them in JS. `pg` may return the id as a number or a string depending on the column type, while `req.params.id` is always a string, so a JS `===` can fail in confusing ways. Postgres handles the type conversion itself.

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

Return 404 rather than 403, so a caller can't use the response to find out which ids exist in other accounts.

## Making it hard to repeat

Every write already goes through `db.write` (`db.js:13`), which runs its own transaction. That makes Postgres row-level security a good backstop:
- Change `write` to take the account id and run `SET LOCAL app.account_id = ...` right after `BEGIN`.
- Add policies on `accounts` and `orders` that compare against `current_setting('app.account_id')`.

With that in place, a future route that forgets the filter gets zero rows instead of another tenant's data. Two things to know:
- RLS is skipped for the table owner unless you also run `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`.
- Reads go through `query()`, which doesn't open a transaction, so they'd need the same setting before RLS covers them.

I'd make the per-query fixes now and treat RLS as a follow-up.

## Other things I noticed

- **Probably nothing gets past auth right now.** `auth.js:8` reads `req.cookies`, but `app.js` never mounts `cookie-parser`. That means the session token is `undefined` and every request gets a 401, unless something outside these five files sets it up. You'll run into this as soon as you try to test the fix.
- **Deleted accounts still work.** `sessionUser` doesn't check `accounts.deleted_at`, so users of a soft-deleted account can still log in and use these routes. The fix is to join `accounts` there and filter on `deleted_at IS NULL`.
- **There's no role check.** After the fix, any member of an account can still rename or delete it. If only admins should be able to do that, you'll need a role on `users`, and `sessionUser` doesn't select one today.

