Both bugs are the same shape: `req.user.accountId` is set by the session middleware (`auth.js:11`) on every request, but the `UPDATE` queries key off `req.params.id` alone, so any authenticated user can mutate another account's data by guessing an id (IDOR).

**`routes/accounts.js:6-18`** — `PATCH /:id` and `DELETE /:id` update by account `id` with no check that it's the caller's own account:
```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.params.id !== String(req.user.accountId)) return res.status(404).end();
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.user.accountId]
  );
  res.json(row);
});
```
Same pattern for `delete`. Since an account row's `id` *is* the account id, scope the query to `req.user.accountId` rather than trusting the URL param (and reject upfront if they don't match, so a mismatched id gives 404 instead of silently no-oping).

**`routes/orders.js:14-20`** — `PATCH /:id/cancel` updates an order by its own `id` with no `account_id` filter:
```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

Both fixes need the accompanying "0 rows returned → 404" check — right now `res.json(row)`/`res.status(204)` fire unconditionally, so once you add scoping, a cross-account request would otherwise return a misleading success with an empty/undefined body instead of a clear 404.

