The natural place is a single Express middleware in `app.js`, following the same pattern `sessionUser` already establishes there ("every request passes through here") — not scattered `console.log` calls inside `accounts.js`/`orders.js`.

### Shape of it

```js
const { randomUUID } = require("crypto");

function requestLog(req, res, next) {
  req.id = req.headers["x-request-id"] || randomUUID();
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();

  res.on("finish", () => {
    const ms = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6;
    log.info({
      reqId: req.id,
      method: req.method,
      path: req.path,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    });
  });

  next();
}
```

Mount it **first**, before `express.json()` and before `sessionUser`:

```js
app.use(requestLog);
app.use(express.json());
app.use(sessionUser);
app.use("/accounts", accounts);
app.use("/orders", orders);
```

Attaching the `res.on("finish", ...)` listener early means it still fires for requests that die before reaching a route (bad JSON body, failed auth in `sessionUser`), and by the time it actually *fires* — after the response is sent — `req.user` will already be populated if the request got that far, so `accountId`/`userId` show up on the log line whenever they're known. `req.id` is generated up front so you can correlate this log entry with anything else that happens during the request (including DB errors, per below).

### What to log / not log

- Log `req.user.id` and `req.user.accountId` for correlation — not `req.user.email` (PII) and never the session cookie/token.
- Don't log `req.body` wholesale — `accounts.js` PATCH carries `billing_email`, which you don't want sitting in log files. If you want body visibility for debugging, log field *names* present, not values, or explicitly allowlist non-sensitive fields.
- Log `res.statusCode` and duration, not full response bodies.

### The gap this doesn't close by itself

This ties back to what we found earlier: neither route handler has a `try/catch`, and `app.js` has no error-handling middleware. In Express 4, a rejected promise from an async handler never calls `res.end()` — so `res.on("finish")` never fires, and that request produces **no log line at all**, right when you'd want one most (it's also the request that hangs the client and, per the unhandled-rejection default, may crash the process). Request logging and the missing error boundary are the same fix in practice: add a 4-arg error middleware (or wrap the async handlers) so every path — success, thrown error, or the `db.write()` COMMIT-ambiguity case from before — always reaches `res.end()`, which is what actually guarantees a log line gets emitted.

Worth also touching `db.write()` itself while you're in there: right now, in the `catch` block, if `ROLLBACK` also throws (the dead-connection case we walked through), that new error silently replaces the original `err` before it's thrown — the real cause never surfaces anywhere, logged or not. Logging `err` at the point it's first caught in `write()` (before the `ROLLBACK` attempt can mask it) would preserve that context and let you tag it with `req.id` if you thread that through, rather than losing it entirely.

